 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:**
-*   Vertex Addition: $O(1)$ amortized (free-list allocation).
-*   Face Removal: $O(V)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices in the mesh, due to linear search in `findFace` [S1, unofficial].
-*   Block Update: $O(1)$ per face (6 faces), but depends on neighbor chunk loading [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Memory Complexity:**
-*   Vertex Storage: 8 bytes per vertex (2x 32-bit uint) [S1, unofficial].
-*   Index Storage: 4 bytes per index.
-*   Buffer Size: Proportional to visible faces. Dynamic updates use `gl.DYNAMIC_DRAW` hint [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Performance:**
-*   Measured on WebGL/OpenGL contexts. Performance depends on chunk size and visibility ratio.
-*   Strengths: Low memory footprint, fast incremental updates, simple data layout.
-*   Limitations: Linear search for face removal is slow for large meshes. No support for complex geometry beyond quads. Precision limits for position (10 bits per axis) restrict world size or block resolution [S1, unofficial].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Voxel terrain with frequent block changes.
-*   Limited memory bandwidth.
-*   Simple block types with fixed geometry.
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Large-scale worlds requiring high-precision coordinates.
-*   Complex, non-voxel geometry.
-*   Static scenes where pre-computed meshes are feasible.
+-   **Time Complexity:** Face culling is $O(1)$ per face. Mesh generation is proportional to the number of visible faces. Buffer updates are $O(V + I)$ where $V$ is vertices and $I$ is indices.
+-   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for vertex data, $O(I)$ for indices. Packing reduces vertex size to 8 bytes per vertex.
+-   **Performance:** Efficient for dynamic voxel worlds with frequent block changes. Reduces GPU memory bandwidth by minimizing buffer uploads.
+-   **Strengths:** Low memory footprint, fast incremental updates, simple implementation.
+-   **Limitations:** Limited coordinate range (10 bits), requires careful management of vertex slots and indices, not suitable for highly complex non-voxel geometry.
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the Exolands project, this method was developed to handle dynamic voxel terrain in web-based games. It addresses the limitations of naive meshing by combining face culling with efficient vertex management. The approach is documented in the Exolands source code [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the Exolands project, an unofficial voxel engine implementation [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the principles of face culling and visibility optimization [3861-face-culling-and-visibility-optimization.md] to create efficient chunk-based meshes.
 
